ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [The "irreversible destruction" reduces to a well-known PIL default: PIL.Image.save() does not carry over the exif dict from in-memory Image objects unless exif= is passed — documented behavior, not a discovered coupling. The "cross-mirror score fork" is just (known PIL strip-on-save) × (known decoder-ignores-EXIF default) × (known phone-roll EXIF prevalence); the product is arithmetic, not a new mechanism. The 42.1pp per-tagged flip is the trivial upper bound for a 90° rotation on an orientation-sensitive CNN — predicted by basic invariance theory, not surprising.]
CONFOUNDS: [Selection of the 140-image main set is not described — if tagged-image subset is non-random w.r.t. ImageNet class difficulty, the 42.1pp flip is inflated. "8 served HF parquet benchmarks scanned → f~0" is presented as evidence of pervasive destruction, but is equally consistent with publishers who simply uploaded already-normalized images (curation, not serialization destruction); no causal attribution to push_to_hub path. Open Images Rotation column (1.3%) vs raw-Flickr re-scan (2.75%) discrepancy unexplained — could indicate prior normalization upstream, confounding the "real f" estimate.]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [That a non-trivial consumer population actually honors EXIF on eval (stress-test #3 — the fork is latent without this, and no measurement of real consumer behavior is offered, only a list of tools that *could* honor it). That push_to_hub PIL-object path is the dominant publish path in practice (not measured; could be rare vs file-path uploads, which preserve). That "invisibility" holds — dataset cards / parquet bytes-hash diffs would in fact reveal divergence to anyone comparing mirrors; "invisible" is asserted, not tested against standard mirror-diff tooling.]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [N=140 main, n_tagged=6 from Open Images raw (per orient hist {1:389, 6:6}) — no confidence interval on the 42.1pp flip given the tiny tagged-n that actually drives the headline; CI on f=2.75% from 400 images is ±~1.6pp (Wilson), which straddles the 5% materiality threshold. No seed/replication variance reported. No model variance — single model (resnet50 V2); orientation-sensitivity is architecture- and augmentation-dependent and a ViT or rotation-augmented model could collapse the 42pp flip substantially. Materiality argument leans on f≥5% regime that the project's own real measurement does not reach.]
RATIONALE: The serialization step IS a real, verified seam (step-0 gate survived in pinned libs, controls held), and the irreversibility + cross-mirror-invisibility framing is a legitimate re-composition not obviously present in the cited robustness literature — that earns it above RED. But the chain decomposes into three individually-known defaults (PIL strip-on-save, decoder-ignores-EXIF, phone-roll EXIF prevalence), and the "new coupling" is their product plus a framing ("irreversible", "invisible") that is partly rhetorical: byte-hash mirror diffs are standard and would expose it. At the measured real f=2.75% the fork is 1.16pp — below the project's own 2pp materiality bar — and the >2pp regime requires assumptions (higher f, EXIF-honoring consumers) that are asserted from literature ranges, not measured in this run. This is the MECHANISM-KNOWN ceiling at altitude: distinct from CLAIM-0059 (tokenizer-normalizer→dedup-recall was a causal coupling between two components neither of which independently predicted the outcome); here both endpoints (strip-on-save, decoder-ignores) independently and obviously predict the outcome once composed. L1 should measure: (a) real distribution of publish-path choice on HF, (b) real consumer-loader EXIF-honoring rate, (c) ≥1 ViT/augmented model to test flip magnitude, (d) tighter CI on f from a larger raw sample — without (a)+(b) the materiality is unestablished and the claim cannot clear the ceiling. Cited prior art: PIL.Image.save EXIF behavior (Pillow docs, stable multi-year); torchvision apply_exif_orientation default False (verified in packet); CloudFactory / arXiv 1911.07201 (rotation footgun, packet-cited).
